ewlabel{tab:prompt_variants}{{14}{19}{BCS--accuracy Pearson correlation across four prompt variants. At count 32, all four variants show negative BCS--accuracy correlations ($r \leq -0.47$), with two reaching $p < 0.05$. At count 8, the narrower BCS range reduces statistical power}{table.caption.55}{}}

ewlabel{sec:appendix_bcs_sensitivity}{{I}{19}{BCS Aggregation Sensitivity}{appendix.I}{}}

ewlabel{tab:bcs_aggregation}{{15}{19}{BCS--accuracy regression under mean vs.\ max pairwise SCS aggregation (FewRel, 16-relation batches). Permutation test for slope equivalence across pools: MEAN $p \approx 0.60$; MAX $p \approx 0.44$. The 80-pool row is identical under both definitions because these batches were collected using mean-aggregated BCS; the MAX column reflects the same data for completeness}{table.caption.56}{}}

ewlabel{app:bcs-controlled-table}{{J}{19}{BCS-Controlled Detailed Results}{appendix.J}{}}

ewlabel{tab:bcs_controlled}{{16}{20}{BCS-controlled FewRel accuracy. Low-BCS batches contain maximally dissimilar types; high-BCS batches contain maximally similar types. The 80-relation extreme-batch gap reflects the wider BCS range; regression slopes are consistent across pools ($p = 0.60$; Table~ef {tab:within_pool}; see \S ef {sec:practical_magnitude} for typical-batch magnitudes). The 16-relation condition used a single extreme batch per BCS level; bootstrap CIs require multiple batches}{table.caption.57}{}}

ewlabel{app:format-independence}{{K}{20}{Format Independence: Paired Constrained vs.\ Unconstrained Comparison}{appendix.K}{}}

ewlabel{tab:format_independence}{{17}{20}{Paired comparison of constrained vs.\ unconstrained decoding on 25 identical FewRel batch type sets. Both formats yield virtually identical BCS--accuracy correlations (Fisher $z \approx 0$), confirming the confusability effect is format-independent. Mean accuracy is also unchanged ($\Delta = -0.08$\,pp), ruling out a format-driven accuracy shift}{table.caption.59}{}}

ewlabel{tab:format_perbatch}{{18}{20}{Per-batch accuracy (\%) under constrained and unconstrained decoding on 25 identical FewRel batch type sets (count 8, 96 instances each). 23/25 batches yield identical accuracy; the two exceptions differ by exactly one sample ($\Delta = -1.04$\,pp). L = low-BCS, H = high-BCS}{table.caption.61}{}}

ewlabel{app:within-pool-method}{{L}{20}{Within-Pool Analysis Methodology}{appendix.L}{}}

ewlabel{tab:within_pool}{{19}{21}{Three-way BCS--accuracy analysis on FewRel. Slope: OLS of batch accuracy on BCS (bootstrap CIs, $B{=}10{,}000$). $\Delta $: accuracy gap between top- and bottom-quartile BCS batches. See Appendix~ef {sec:appendix_within_pool} for CI interpretation}{table.caption.62}{}}

ewlabel{app:scs-ablation}{{M}{21}{SCS Embedding Ablation: Name-Only vs. Name+Definition}{appendix.M}{}}

ewlabel{tab:scs-ablation}{{20}{21}{Pearson correlation between SCS and empirical confusion rate. ``Def'' concatenates type name with definition before embedding. All correlations significant ($p < 0.001$). The inconsistent direction of $\Delta r$ indicates that definitions provide no systematic benefit}{table.caption.63}{}}

ewlabel{app:scs-heldout-validation}{{M}{21}{Held-out validation}{section*.64}{}}

ewlabel{app:aggregation-sim}{{N}{21}{Pair-to-Batch Aggregation Simulation}{appendix.N}{}}

ewlabel{app:bcs-composition}{{O}{22}{BCS Independence from Type Composition}{appendix.O}{}}

ewlabel{app:scs-baselines}{{P}{22}{SCS vs.\ Simple Baselines}{appendix.P}{}}

ewlabel{tab:scs_baselines}{{21}{22}{Pairwise confusion prediction: SCS vs.\ simple baselines. Spearman $ho $ between each metric and the empirical confusion rate. SCS outperforms all baselines on FewRel; the margin is smaller on Few-NERD, where hierarchical naming conventions provide partial signal to string-based metrics. $^\dagger $42\% of pairs lack WordNet coverage}{table.caption.70}{}}

ewlabel{app:split-origin}{{Q}{22}{Split-Origin Covariate Analysis}{appendix.Q}{}}

ewlabel{app:variance-decomposition}{{R}{23}{Cross-Seed Variance Decomposition}{appendix.R}{}}

ewlabel{tab:variance_decomposition}{{22}{23}{Variance decomposition on FewRel (3 seeds $	imes $ 25 batches per count level). Batch SD: root-mean intra-seed variance averaged over seeds. Seed SD: standard deviation of per-seed means. Ratio = batch variance / seed variance. At count 80, only one batch composition exists, so batch variance vanishes by construction}{table.caption.73}{}}

ewlabel{app:cross-domain}{{S}{23}{Cross-Domain Connections}{appendix.S}{}}

ewlabel{sec:appendix_cross_model_70b}{{T}{23}{Cross-Model Validation with LLaMA-3.1-70B-Instruct (FP8)}{appendix.T}{}}

ewlabel{tab:cross_model_70b}{{23}{23}{Cross-model count curves for LLaMA-3.1-70B-Instruct (FP8) and Qwen3-32B. RE decline (count 8$	o $80): $-$30.7\,pp (70B) vs.\ $-$30.1\,pp (32B). NER decline (count 2$	o $22): $-$7.1\,pp (70B) vs.\ $-$23.6\,pp (32B); both models exhibit a U-shaped rebound at higher counts. 25 batches per count level, same samples and batch compositions across models (seed 42). All metrics are micro-averaged across batches}{table.caption.74}{}}

ewlabel{sec:appendix_cross_model_gemini}{{U}{24}{Cross-Model Validation with Gemini 2.5 Flash (API)}{appendix.U}{}}

ewlabel{tab:cross_model_gemini}{{24}{24}{Cross-model count curves for Gemini 2.5 Flash (API) and Qwen3-32B on FewRel RE (classification accuracy). 25 batches per count level, same samples and batch compositions (seed 42). Gemini's count-1 result is included for completeness; Qwen3 count-1 was not collected. Both models show monotonic decline from count 4 onward ($ho = -1.0$ for both on counts 4--32). $^\dagger $Count 64: Gemini based on 11/25 batches (partial)}{table.caption.75}{}}

ewlabel{tab:cross_model_bcs_summary}{{25}{24}{Cross-model BCS-controlled FewRel RE summary. All models show significant low-vs-high BCS accuracy gaps. Gap magnitude varies with model capacity (ceiling compression), but the direction is consistent across open-weight (8B--70B) and proprietary architectures}{table.caption.77}{}}

ewlabel{sec:appendix_fewshot_bcs}{{V}{24}{Few-Shot Prompting and Confusability}{appendix.V}{}}

ewlabel{tab:fewshot_bcs}{{26}{25}{BCS gap under few-shot prompting (FewRel, count 8). Demonstrations improve absolute accuracy on low-BCS batches (+3.3--7.2\,pp over 0-shot) but provide inconsistent benefit on high-BCS batches (+5.6\,pp at 1-shot, $-$1.0\,pp at 5-shot). The confusability gap widens from +18.5\,pp (0-shot) to +22.8\,pp (5-shot). All metrics are micro-averaged across batches}{table.caption.78}{}}

ewlabel{app:error-analysis}{{W}{25}{Qualitative Error Analysis}{appendix.W}{}}

ewlabel{tab:top10_re}{{27}{25}{Top-10 confused relation pairs on FewRel 80-relation RE. $N$: bidirectional confusion count aggregated over 3 seeds $	imes $ 25 batches. SCS: pairwise Schema Confusability Score (Qwen3 embedding cosine similarity). High-SCS pairs (e.g., 	extit {followed by}/	extit {follows}, SCS\,=\,0.90) and family-relation pairs (	extit {child}/	extit {father}, 	extit {child}/	extit {mother}) dominate. Some low-SCS pairs (	extit {instance of}/	extit {taxon rank}, SCS\,=\,0.53) reflect domain-specific co-occurrence rather than semantic similarity}{table.caption.79}{}}

ewlabel{app:renaming}{{X}{25}{Schema Label Renaming Experiment}{appendix.X}{}}

ewlabel{tab:top10_ner}{{28}{26}{Top-10 confused entity type pairs on Few-NERD NER. $N$: bidirectional confusion count from 66-type full-schema extraction. SCS: pairwise Schema Confusability Score (Qwen3 embedding cosine similarity). Intra-category confusions (e.g., 	extit {person-other}/	extit {person-politician}, SCS\,=\,0.93) and cross-category pairs sharing referent overlap (	extit {building-other}/	extit {location-GPE}, SCS\,=\,0.85) are most frequent. The ``other'' subtypes absorb diverse entities and confuse with more specific siblings}{table.caption.80}{}}

ewlabel{app:relaxed-matching}{{Y}{26}{Relaxed Matching Evaluation (NER)}{appendix.Y}{}}

ewlabel{tab:error_examples}{{29}{27}{Illustrative confusion instances from FewRel 80-rel RE. All gold-predicted pairs have SCS\,$>$\,0.90: 	extit {participant} vs.\ 	extit {participant of} reverses directionality; 	extit {contains} vs.\ 	extit {located in} inverts containment; 	extit {part of} vs.\ 	extit {member of} conflates meronymy with membership. a.t.e.\ = admin.\ territorial entity}{table.caption.81}{}}

ewlabel{tab:renaming}{{30}{27}{Accuracy (\%) under original vs.\ renamed labels on FewRel count-8 batches. Renaming reduced embedding similarity but decreased accuracy in both groups, with a larger drop on high-BCS batches (Cohen's $d = -2.92$) than low-BCS ($d = -1.09$). Interaction: $\Delta _{	ext {high}} - \Delta _{	ext {low}} = -5.9$\,pp ($t = -7.23$, $p < 0.001$)}{table.caption.82}{}}

ewlabel{tab:relaxed_matching}{{31}{27}{BCS--F1 correlation under progressively relaxed NER matching. Relaxed span credits a match if the predicted span overlaps the gold span and the type is correct; token overlap uses token-level intersection; type-ignored drops the type constraint entirely. Relaxed matching improves mean F1 by 4.2\,pp (98.4\% of batches improve), but Pearson $r$ remains near zero across all modes (bootstrap 95\% CI crosses zero in all cases). Boundary errors affect all batches uniformly and do not mask a BCS signal}{table.caption.83}{}}

